UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 1: Point of view: first and third person', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Point of view is who tells the story: first person uses I/we from inside a character; third person uses he/she/they and can follow one or more characters from outside.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nLabel each sentence first or third: (1) We hid behind the crate. (2) Owen checked the tide chart twice.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position.\n\nCHECK\nMixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRewrite a short third-person paragraph into first person (or the reverse). Keep the same events. Highlight pronoun changes and explain one effect on how close the reader feels to the character.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Point of view is who tells the story: first person uses I/we from inside a character; third person uses he/she/they and can follow one or more characters from outside.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rewrite a short third-person paragraph into first person (or the reverse). Keep the same events. Highlight pronoun changes and explain one effect on how close the reader feels to the character. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: (1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position. Reasoning: I used point of view by attending to first person rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in. Correction: (1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position. Why: the correction restores accurate use of point of view.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating point of view on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with point of view, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"(1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position. This matches the lesson skill for point of view.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","(1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position.","Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Label each sentence first or third: (1) We hid behind the crate. (2) Owen checked the tide chart twice. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in.","Point of view is who tells the story: first person uses I/we from inside a character; third person uses he/she/they and can follow one or more characters from…","(1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position.","Careful use of first person with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising point of view?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named first person, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the first person used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising point of view. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries point of view.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show point of view and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses point of view from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","(1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position.","Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about point of view?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise first person?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about point of view."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of point of view to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.",