},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFirst: \"I gripped the wet rope and hoped the boat would steady.\" Third: \"Mia gripped the wet rope and hoped the boat would steady.\" Same actions; different narrator position.\n\nTRY FIRST\nLabel each sentence first or third: (1) We hid behind the crate. (2) Owen checked the tide chart twice.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(1) first person; (2) third person. Pronouns signal the narrator’s position.","explain":"Point of view is who tells the story: first person uses I/we from inside a character; third person uses he/she/they and can follow one or more characters from outside.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Mixing I and she for the same narrator without a clear reason confuses whose eyes we are in. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Same action told in first and third person.","caption":"Pronouns signal narrator position.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First person</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I gripped the rope</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Third person</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Mia gripped th