ccuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about point of view?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPoint of view is who tells the story: first person uses I/we from inside a character; third person uses he/she/they and can follow one or more characters from outside. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 1: Point of view: first and third person","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: suspense, point of view and voice","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nFirst: \"I gripped the wet rope and hoped the boat would steady.\" Third: \"Mia gripped the wet rope and hoped the boat would steady.\" Same actions; different narrator position.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Same action told in first and third person.","caption":"Pronouns signal narrator position.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First person</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I gripped the rope</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Third person</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Mia gripped the rope</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Closeness effect contrasted with confusing mixes.","caption":"Keep point of view steady unless shifting on purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Effect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">closer inside I</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">mixed I and she</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y4-w02-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
